    "timestamp": "2026-06-10T00:12:20.874647Z",
    "schema_version": 1,
    "seq": 5,
    "meta": {},
    "summary": "Scaffold a minimal Vite + vanilla-JS app showing \"BP03 contract\" and start the Vite dev server on port 8000.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "title": "Initialize Vite project with vanilla-JS template",
        "detail": "Run `npm create vite@latest . -- --template vanilla` to scaffold the project in the workspace root, then install dependencies."
      },
      {
        "title": "Set the heading to 'BP03 contract'",
        "detail": "Edit the generated main.js (or index.html) so the page renders an <h1> with the text 'BP03 contract'."
      },
      {
        "title": "Start Vite dev server on port 8000",
        "detail": "Run `npx vite --port 8000 --host` so the live preview is the Vite dev server, not a static build."
      }
    ],
    "revision": 1,
    "context": "## Findings\n- The workspace is completely empty \u2014 no existing files or config.\n\n## Approach\n1. **Scaffold** using Vite's official `vanilla` template, which gives us a minimal setup (index.html + main.js) with no framework overhead.\n2. **Customize** the content to show the required heading.\n3. **Run the dev server** on port 8000 so the user preview is live and reflects the Vite dev server (with HMR), not a static build output.\n\n## Trade-offs\n- Using `npm create vite` means we need Node.js/npm available in the sandbox (standard for Vite projects).\n- The dev server approach means the preview stays live and updates automatically on file changes."
  },
